        "text": "band fades.\" and tweeter, showing where each speaker hands off to the next. Tool 3: Time alignment This is the tool most people have never heard of, and often the one that changes the experience most. Sound travels at roughly 343 meters per second in air, or about 1 foot per millisecond. If the left door woofer is 2.5 feet from your ears and the right one is 4 feet away, sound from the right arrives roughly 1.3 milliseconds later. That sounds trivial, but our hearing uses arrival time to locate sound. Time alignment delays the signal to the nearer speakers so that sound from every speaker arrives at the listening position at the same moment. The effect is that the stereo image moves from the near door to the center of the dash, and instruments spread out across the windshield in front of you. with distances labeled (illustrative values). Left panel \"Before\": arrival times staggered, sound image pulled left. Right panel \"After\": delays applied to nearer speakers (shown as small clock icons with ms values), arrivals synchronized, image centered on the dash. the center of the dash. arrival from each speaker with synchronized arrival after time alignment. Time alignment is set for a listening position. It can be optimized for the driver, for both front seats as a compromise, or saved as different presets. That trade-off should be a conversation with the owner, not a default. Tool 4: Equalization Equalization (EQ) adjusts the level of specific frequency ranges. In a car, its main job is correcting the peaks and dips that the cabin and speaker positions create at the listening position. A DSP typically offers parametric EQ: each adjustment has a center frequency, an amount of boost or cut, and a width (often called Q). That precision lets a tuner target a specific resonance without affecting neighboring frequencies. Many processors allow EQ per channel, so each speaker can be corrected individually before the system is tuned as a whole. Good EQ practice has limits:"
